38 #edb/ref 17592186423589 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423597 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423590 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423591 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423593 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423596 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423598 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423587 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423592 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423600 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186133982 1050 480.0 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186134239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186134239 1071 "If $‖3x‖ = 4$, find $‖\\frac{x}{5}‖$." 13194139534508 17592186045417 false] [17592186134239 1071 "If $‖3x‖=4$, find $‖\\frac{x}{5}‖.$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186134239 1074 "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n‖kx‖=|k|\\cdot ‖x‖\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖3x‖ & =4 \\\\ |3|\\cdot ‖x‖ & =4 \\\\ ‖x‖ & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $‖\\frac{x}{5}‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\frac{x}{5}‖ & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186135031 1064 "The vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. \n\nFor a vector ![](images/83/838ca0a3284985fa58d5ac894740b15f9bbbfd931299834cc8e062e364bbf983.svg) in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$  the **norm** (or **length**)  is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}} = \\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2+\\ldots+x_n^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector ![](images/2a/2a4e0070bdec6033ab8b71cbb77dbd8569bea8672c0e17cedbf876a77ce56bb2.svg) The norm of this vector is calculated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the dot product of a vector with itself is always non-negative, $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x}\\geq 0,$ so it's always possible to take the square root $\\sqrt{ \\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} }.$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186135031 1064 "The vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ paired with the dot product, is called a **Euclidean space**. \n\nFor a vector $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$  the **norm** (or **length**)  is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}} = \\sqrt{x_1^2+x_2^2+\\ldots+x_n^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]$ The norm of this vector is calculated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the dot product of a vector with itself is always non-negative, $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x}\\geq 0,$ so it's always possible to take the square root $\\sqrt{ \\mathbf{x}\\cdot\\mathbf{x} }.$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 false] [17592186135073 1064 "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nTo create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can express the norm in terms of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+⋯+({x}_{n}-{y}_{n})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-(-5))^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 false] [17592186135073 1064 "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nTo create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:\n![](images/01/011499b685af9a4c78d195755dc34e7b0d6ea0292eb2e9feafb94312fc7ee4b3.svg) \n\nNow, we can express the norm in terms of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+⋯+({x}_{n}-{y}_{n})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors ![](images/2e/2e5d7cbac29024ca7f343363598714ea17bb180036d2f50f094a25584be2e23e.svg) and ![](images/37/37364e71c36f25701aa2e3b0ad368d4567b5719aa0c884ff0406ee9686125808.svg) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-(-5))^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186135074 1064 "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector ![](images/43/430769431d512b87b6e67712fd8bd8de3802688f883600602a0d45b2749089cc.svg) To compute the norm of a multiple of $\\mathbf{x},$ say ${\\color{blue}-2}\\mathbf{x},$ we can factor the absolute value $|{\\color{blue}-2}|$ out of the norm as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =|-2|\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ we have\n\n![](images/5d/5da07173bda8f167d9a0609457fd10f1e3fdcdd2de498fd08c1c0035a205e5e1.svg)\n\nThen, computing the norm, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186135074 1064 "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ To compute the norm of a multiple of $\\mathbf{x},$ say ${\\color{blue}-2}\\mathbf{x},$ we can factor the absolute value $|{\\color{blue}-2}|$ out of the norm as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =|-2|\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, computing the norm, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534508 17592186045417 false] [17592186423503 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423503 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423503 1083 #uuid "0301abe0-3c1d-5d0f-ba9c-e0b6f7e434e4" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423504 1080 "5" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423504 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423504 1083 #uuid "0321dad9-7a91-5b43-b252-10b7425bf47e" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423505 1080 "2" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423505 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423505 1083 #uuid "04dbab12-ca8b-5d27-a241-6617b3607e24" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423506 1080 "6" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423506 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423506 1083 #uuid "06a5fc03-9e34-57fb-8e35-1287e254f707" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423507 1080 "4\\sqrt{5}" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423507 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423507 1083 #uuid "09cd35ab-5375-5955-b3eb-b86578cb2956" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423508 1080 "k=3\\pm \\sqrt{13}" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423508 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423508 1083 #uuid "0b0ef00b-610f-581a-8e67-24f68a95faf0" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423509 1080 "40" 13194139534508 17592186045417 true] [17592186423509 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534508 17592186045417